Below is the complete step-by-step process to configure GitHub Actions → Azure authentication using OIDC (OpenID Connect) without storing any client secret. This is Microsoft's recommended approach.

Architecture
GitHub Actions
       │
       │ Requests OIDC Token
       ▼
GitHub OIDC Provider
       │
       ▼
Microsoft Entra ID
(App Registration + Federated Credential)
       │
       ▼
Azure Subscription
       │
       ▼
Terraform Deployment
Prerequisites

You need:

Azure Subscription
Azure CLI installed
GitHub repository
Repository Admin permission
Azure Owner or User Access Administrator role
Step 1: Login to Azure
az login

Check the subscription:

az account show

If necessary, select the correct subscription:

az account set --subscription "<subscription-name>"
Step 2: Create an App Registration

Azure Portal:

Azure Portal
        │
        ▼
Microsoft Entra ID
        │
        ▼
App registrations
        │
        ▼
New registration

Example:

Name

github-terraform-oidc

Click Register.

Step 3: Copy Important Values

From the Overview page, copy:

Application (Client) ID

Directory (Tenant) ID

Also copy the Subscription ID:

Subscriptions
      │
      ▼
Overview

Save these values.

Step 4: Create a Service Principal

Using Azure CLI:

az ad sp create \
  --id <CLIENT_ID>

Example:

az ad sp create \
  --id 12345678-abcd-1234-abcd-1234567890ab
Step 5: Assign RBAC Permissions

Assign the Contributor role (or a more restrictive role if appropriate):

az role assignment create \
  --assignee <CLIENT_ID> \
  --role Contributor \
  --scope /subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>

For production, consider assigning permissions only at the resource group scope.

Step 6: Configure the Federated Credential

Azure Portal:

Microsoft Entra ID
      │
      ▼
App registrations
      │
