github-terraform-oidc
      │
      ▼
Certificates & secrets
      │
      ▼
Federated credentials

Click:

Add credential
Step 7: Choose GitHub Actions

Select:

Federated credential scenario

GitHub Actions
Step 8: Enter GitHub Details

Suppose your repository is:

Owner

gopal1409

Repository

skywalker-terraform

Choose:

Entity Type

Branch

Branch:

master

or

main

depending on your default branch.

Name:

github-master

Azure generates the subject automatically:

repo:gopal1409/skywalker-terraform:ref:refs/heads/master

This subject must match the repository and branch your workflow runs from.

Click Add.

Step 9: Create GitHub Secrets

In your repository:

Settings
      │
      ▼
Secrets and variables
      │
      ▼
Actions

Create only these three secrets:

Secret	Value
AZURE_CLIENT_ID	Application (Client) ID
AZURE_TENANT_ID	Tenant ID
AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID	Subscription ID

Do not create AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET. OIDC does not use a client secret.

Step 10: Update the GitHub Workflow

Enable OIDC permissions:

permissions:
  id-token: write
  contents: read

Authenticate with Azure:

- name: Azure Login
  uses: azure/login@v3

  with:
    client-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}
    tenant-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}
    subscription-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}

No client-secret and no creds input are required for OIDC.

Step 11: Verify Login

Add:

- name: Verify Login
  run: az account show

Expected output:

{
  "environmentName": "AzureCloud",
  "subscriptionId": "...",
  "tenantId": "..."
}
Step 12: Configure the AzureRM Provider

For AzureRM Provider 4.x:

provider "azurerm" {
  features {}

  subscription_id = var.subscription_id
}

Define the variable:

variable "subscription_id" {
  type = string
}

Pass it in the workflow:

- name: Terraform Plan
  env:
    TF_VAR_subscription_id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}
  run: terraform plan -out=tfplan
Step 13: Complete Workflow Example
name: Terraform OIDC

on:
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  id-token: write
  contents: read

jobs:

  terraform:

    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:

      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3

      - name: Azure Login
        uses: azure/login@v3
        with:
          client-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}
          tenant-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}
          subscription-id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}

      - name: Verify Login
        run: az account show

      - name: Terraform Init
        run: terraform init

      - name: Terraform Plan
        env:
          TF_VAR_subscription_id: ${{ secrets.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}
        run: terraform plan
Common Errors
Error	Cause	Resolution
AADSTS70025	No federated credential configured	Add a federated credential in the App Registration with the correct repository and branch.
AADSTS70021	Subject mismatch	Ensure the branch in the federated credential exactly matches the workflow branch.
Unexpected input client-secret	Using OIDC with client-secret	Remove client-secret and use only client-id, tenant-id, and subscription-id.
subscription_id is required	AzureRM provider v4.x	Set subscription_id in the provider configuration and pass it to Terraform.
Final Authentication Flow
GitHub Workflow
        │
        ▼
GitHub Issues OIDC Token
        │
        ▼
Microsoft Entra ID
        │
Checks:
• Repository
• Branch
• Workflow Identity
        │
        ▼
Temporary Azure Access Token
        │
        ▼
Azure Login Action
        │
        ▼
Terraform Init
        │
        ▼
Terraform Plan
        │
        ▼
Terraform Apply

This approach is more secure than storing service principal secrets because GitHub requests a short-lived token for each workflow run instead of using long-lived credentials.
